<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>2.4 Solving the heat equation</title>

  <p>
    <alert>MTH 401</alert>
  </p>

  <remark>
    <p>
      General steps to solving the heat equation:
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Step 1:</title>

          <p>
            Is it a homogeneous equation with homogeneous boundary conditions?
            If not, make it so.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Step 2:</title>

          <p>
            Separation of variables.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Step 3:</title>

          <p>
            Identify and solve the Sturm-Lioville problem.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Step 4:</title>

          <p>
            Solve temporal differential equation, write product solutions, use
            superposition to write general solution.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <title>Step 5:</title>

          <p>
            Find Fourier Coefficients.
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <p>
    Here are some examples of the heat equation along with boundary and initial
    conditions.
    Note, they each have the same <m>\alpha</m> (same materials) and describe a
    beam of length <m>5</m>, but they have different boundary and initial
    conditions.
    Find a solution for each and graph it using Mathematica (recommended, can
    use other technology).
  </p>

  <exercise workspace="3in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <md>
          \begin{aligned}
    \pd{u}{t} \amp  = 4 \pdtwo{u}{x} \\
    u(0, t) \amp  = 0 \\
    u(5, t) \amp  = 0 \\
    u(x, 0) \amp  = 3\sin\left(\frac{\pi x}{5}\right)
  \end{aligned}
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S2-4-soln">
      <p>
        General solution (which we have previously found for the heat equation
        with fixed boundary conditions):
        <md>
          u(x,t) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n e^{-4\left(\frac{n\pi}{5}\right)^2 t} \sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{5}\right).
        </md>
        Now the initial temperature for <m>t=0</m>, plug it in:
        <md>
          u(x,0) = 3\sin\left(\frac{\pi x}{5}\right) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n (1) \sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{5}\right).
        </md>
        Note the left side is a sine term of <m>\frac{1\pi x}{5}</m> and the
        right side is a bunch of sine terms of <m>\frac{n\pi x}{5}</m>.
        The only way this is true is if <m>n=1</m> survives and all else
        vanishes.
        In particular, <m>A_1 = 3</m> and <m>A_n = 0</m> for
        <m>n=2, 3, \ldots</m>.
        Thus the specific solution is
        <md>
          u(x,t) = 3 e^{-4\left(\frac{1\pi}{5}\right)^2 t} \sin\left(\frac{1\pi x}{5}\right).
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <md>
          \begin{aligned}
    \pd{u}{t} \amp  = 4 \pdtwo{u}{x} \\
    u(0, t) \amp  = 0 \\
    u(5, t) \amp  = 0 \\
    u(x, 0) \amp  = x^2 - 5x
  \end{aligned}
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S2-4-soln">
      <p>
        General solution (which we have previously found for the heat equation
        with fixed boundary conditions):
        <md>
          u(x,t) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n e^{-4\left(\frac{n\pi}{5}\right)^2 t} \sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{5}\right).
        </md>
        Now the initial temperature for <m>t=0</m>, plug it in:
        <md>
          u(x,0) = x^2 - 5x = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n (1) \sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{5}\right).
        </md>
        This is a Fourier Sine Series with Fourier Sine Coefficients:
        <md>
          A_n = \frac25 \int_0^5 (x^2 - 5x)\sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{5}\right)~dx = \frac{100(-1+(-1)^n)}{n^3\pi^3}.
        </md>
        Thus the specific solution is
        <md>
          u(x,t) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{100(-1+(-1)^n)}{n^3\pi^3} e^{-4\left(\frac{n\pi}{5}\right)^2 t} \sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{5}\right).
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <md>
          \begin{aligned}
    \pd{u}{t} \amp  = 4 \pdtwo{u}{x} \\
    \pd{u}{x}(0, t) \amp  = 0 \\
    \pd{u}{x}(5, t) \amp  = 0 \\
    u(x, 0) \amp  = x
  \end{aligned}
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S2-4-soln">
      <p>
        General solution (which we have previously found for the heat equation
        with insulated boundary conditions (NOT FIXED!)):
        <md>
          u(x,t) = A_0 + \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n e^{-4\left(\frac{n\pi}{5}\right)^2 t} \cos\left(\frac{n\pi x}{5}\right).
        </md>
        Now the initial temperature for <m>t=0</m>, plug it in:
        <md>
          u(x,0) = x = A_0 + \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n (1) \cos\left(\frac{n\pi x}{5}\right).
        </md>
        This is a Fourier Cosine Series with Fourier Cosine Coefficients:
        <md>
          <mrow>A_0 \amp = \frac15 \int_0^5 x~dx = \frac52 </mrow>
          <mrow>A_n \amp = \frac25 \int_0^5 (x)\cos\left(\frac{n\pi x}{5}\right)~dx = \frac{10(-1+(-1)^n)}{n^2\pi^2}.</mrow>
        </md>
        Thus the specific solution is
        <md>
          u(x,t) = \frac52+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{10(-1+(-1)^n)}{n^2\pi^2} e^{-4\left(\frac{n\pi}{5}\right)^2 t} \cos\left(\frac{n\pi x}{5}\right).
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <md>
          \begin{aligned}
    \pd{u}{t} \amp  = 4 \pdtwo{u}{x} + 8\\
    u(0, t) \amp  = 0 \\
    u(5, t) \amp  = 0 \\
    u(x, 0) \amp  = 5x - x^2 
  \end{aligned}
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S2-4-soln">
      <p>
        This is not homogeneous because there is a non-zero source term in our
        PDE.
        So we need to find the steady-state solution <m>u_{ss}</m> which is when
        <m>{\pd{u_ss}{t} = 0}</m>.
        According to our PDE, this means <m>{0 = \pdtwo{u_{ss}}{x}+8}</m>, or in
        other words <m>{\pdtwo{u_{ss}}{x} = -2}</m>.
        Integrating up once gives <m>{\pd{u_{ss}}{x} = -2x + A}</m> and
        integrating again gives <m>u_{ss} = -x^2 + Ax + B</m>.
        Plugging in the boundary conditions: <m>{u_{ss}(0,t) = 0 = B}</m> and
        <m>{u_{ss}(5,t) = 0 = -25+5A}</m> so <m>{A=5}</m>.
        So the steady-state solution is
        <md>
          u_{ss} = -x^2+5x.
        </md>
        Define <m>v = u-u_{ss}</m>.
        This will give us the homogeneous PDE with homogeneous B.C.:
        <md>
          <mrow> \pd{v}{t} \amp = 4 \pdtwo{v}{x} </mrow>
          <mrow>v(0,t) \amp = 0 </mrow>
          <mrow>v(5,t) \amp = 0 </mrow>
          <mrow>v(x,0) \amp  = u(x,0) = u_{ss}(x,0) =5x-x^2 - (-x^2+5x) = 0</mrow>
        </md>
        We can find the general solution and all that jazz, but the initial
        temperature is 0, which means when we go to solve the Fourier Sine
        Series we will be taking
        <md>
          A_n = \frac25 \int_{0}^5 (0)~dx = 0
        </md>
        for all <m>n</m>.
        And so <m>v(x,t) = 0</m>.
        That means that <m>{u(x,t) = v(x,t) + u_{ss}(x,t) = 0+(-x^2+5x)}</m> or
        our specific solution is actually the steady-state and doesn't change in
        time!
        <m>u(x,t) = -x^2+5x</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <md>
          \begin{aligned}
    \pd{u}{t} \amp  = 4 \pdtwo{u}{x} + 8\\
    u(0, t) \amp  = 0 \\
    u(5, t) \amp  = 0 \\
    u(x, 0) \amp  = 0
  \end{aligned}
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution component="S2-4-soln">
      <p>
        This is not homogeneous because there is a non-zero source term in our
        PDE.
        So we need to find the steady-state solution <m>u_{ss}</m> which is when
        <m>{\pd{u_ss}{t} = 0}</m>.
        We just did this in the previous problem, finding the steady-state
        solution is
        <md>
          u_{ss} = -x^2+5x.
        </md>
        Define <m>v = u-u_{ss}</m>.
        This will give us the homogeneous PDE with homogeneous B.C.:
        <md>
          <mrow> \pd{v}{t} \amp = 4 \pdtwo{v}{x} </mrow>
          <mrow>v(0,t) \amp = 0 </mrow>
          <mrow>v(5,t) \amp = 0 </mrow>
          <mrow>v(x,0) \amp  = u(x,0) = u_{ss}(x,0) = 0 - (-x^2+5x) = x^2-5x</mrow>
        </md>
        The only difference between this and the last problem is the initial
        temperature, which is no longer zero.
        General solution (which we have previously found for the heat equation
        with fixed boundary conditions):
        <md>
          v(x,t) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n e^{-4\left(\frac{n\pi}{5}\right)^2 t} \sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{5}\right).
        </md>
        Now the initial temperature for <m>t=0</m>, plug it in:
        <md>
          v(x,0) = x^2 - 5x = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n (1) \sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{5}\right).
        </md>
        This is a Fourier Sine Series with Fourier Sine Coefficients we already
        found in part (b):
        <md>
          A_n = \frac25 \int_0^5 (x^2 - 5x)\sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{5}\right)~dx = \frac{100(-1+(-1)^n)}{n^3\pi^3}.
        </md>
        So
        <md>
          v(x,t) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{100(-1+(-1)^n)}{n^3\pi^3} e^{-4\left(\frac{n\pi}{5}\right)^2 t} \sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{5}\right).
        </md>
        But we want <m>u(x,t)</m>.
        Since <m>u(x,t) = v(x,t) + u_{ss}</m> (and I'll put the <m>u_{ss}</m>
        first for easier reading), the final specific solution is
        <md>
          u(x,t) = -x^2 + 5x + \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{100(-1+(-1)^n)}{n^3\pi^3} e^{-4\left(\frac{n\pi}{5}\right)^2 t} \sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{5}\right).
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>